      "dom_id": "question-93081",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "The weight of ore and the weight of copper obtained from it, in kilograms, is shown in the following table:\n\n| Ore | Copper |\n| --- | --- |\n| $423$ | $68$ |\n\nThe ratio of the weight of ore to the weight of copper is always the same.  By rounding the given data to the nearest ten, approximate the weight of copper that can be obtained from $1\\,200$ kilograms of ore.",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's round the weights of ore and copper:\n\n- Ore: $423⟶420$\n\n- Copper: $68⟶70$\n\nTherefore, the ratio of ore to copper (expressed as a fraction) is approximately\n\n$$\n\\frac{420}{70}\n$$\n\nSo the numerator represents the weight of ore, and the denominator represents the weight of copper.\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers by $10$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{420÷10}{70÷10}=\\frac{42}{7}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide by $7$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{42÷7}{7÷7}=\\frac{6}{1}\n$$\n\nWe can make a numerator of $1\\,200$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $200$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{6\\times 200}{1\\times 200}=\\frac{1\\,200}{200}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $200$ kilograms of copper can be obtained from $1\\,200$ kilograms of ore.",
